    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-7-1537-2007",
    "abstract": "Abstract. We briefly present in this short paper a new SIze REsolved Aerosol Model (SIREAM) which simulates the evolution of atmospheric aerosol by solving the General Dynamic Equation (GDE). SIREAM segregates the aerosol size distribution into sections and solves the GDE by splitting coagulation and condensation/evaporation-nucleation. A quasi-stationary sectional approach is used to describe the size distribution change due to condensation/evaporation, and a hybrid equilibrium/dynamical mass-transfer method has been developed to lower the computational burden. SIREAM uses the same physical parameterizations as those used in the Modal Aerosol Model, MAM Sartelet et al. (2006). It is hosted in the modeling system Polyphemus Mallet et al., 2007, but can be linked to any other three-dimensional Chemistry-Transport Model.",
    "field": "Atmospheric aerosol modeling",
    "problem": "Accurately simulating aerosol dynamics is computationally expensive and numerically stiff, especially for condensation/evaporation processes, which complicates the detailed and efficient modeling of atmospheric particulate matter within three-dimensional chemistry-transport models.",
    "method": "The proposed SIze REsolved Aerosol Model (SIREAM) solves the General Dynamic Equation for atmospheric aerosol by segregating the size distribution into sections and applying a splitting approach to separately solve for coagulation and condensation/evaporation, using a hybrid equilibrium/dynamical method to lower computational cost.",
    "task": "The task is to present a new SIze REsolved Aerosol Model (SIREAM) that simulates the evolution of atmospheric aerosols by numerically solving the General Dynamic Equation (GDE) for processes like coagulation, condensation, evaporation, and nucleation.",
